.TH GERANT\-BOOT 8 2026-11-05 garnish "garnish manual"
.SH NAME
gerant\-boot \- sandboxed helper that finds the kernel in a garnish VM's disk image
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gerant\-boot
.RB [ \-\-list ]
.BI < image
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR gerantd (1)
runs
.B gerant\-boot
every time a VM without
.B [Boot] Kernel=
starts or reboots, so that the guest boots the kernel it installed itself.
The disk image is its standard input.
.PP
It reads the image's GPT, then the FAT file systems of its EFI system
partition and, if there is one, its extended boot loader partition
(XBOOTLDR), and looks for kernels where the Boot Loader Specification
puts them:
.IP \(bu 2
unified kernel images,
.IR /EFI/Linux/*.efi ,
whose
.B .linux
and
.B .initrd
sections are the kernel and initrd and whose
.B .uname
section is the kernel's version,
.IP \(bu
type 1 entries,
.IR /loader/entries/*.conf ,
with their
.BR linux ,
.BR initrd ,
.B version
and
.B architecture
lines (or a
.B uki
line naming a unified kernel image).
.PP
Kernels that aren't x86-64 bzImages, entries for other architectures and
entries whose boot counter has run out
.RI ( name+0\- n )
are skipped or tried last. Of the rest, the one with the newest version
wins, compared as
.BR systemd\-boot (7)
compares them; the version comes from the entry or image, or else from
the kernel itself. The command line and other sections in the image are
ignored:
.B gerantd
passes its own command line.
.PP
It writes the chosen kernel and its initrds (one after the other, each
padded to 4 bytes) to standard output, after a short header with the
entry's name, the version and the two sizes. Kernels larger than 64 MiB
and initrds larger than 512 MiB are refused.
.B gerantd
copies them to
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/garnish/name/vmlinuz " and " initrd
and hands those to crosvm.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-list
Lists the kernels found instead, the one that would be booted first: the
partition and path, the version, and the sizes.
.SH SANDBOX
Everything in the image comes from the guest, so before reading any of it
.B gerant\-boot
closes every descriptor but its standard input, output and error, limits
its address space to 512 MiB and its CPU time to 30 seconds, turns off
core dumps and new privileges, and installs a seccomp filter. The filter
lets it read standard input, write standard output and error, manage its
memory and exit; any other system call, such as opening a file or a
socket, kills it.
.B gerantd
gives it 60 seconds, checks the sizes it sends and that the kernel is a
bzImage.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when it sent (or listed) a kernel, 2 when the image has none, 1 on
errors. The reason for an error is the last line on standard error;
earlier lines are warnings about kernels that were skipped, which
.B gerantd
logs.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ gerant\-boot \-\-list < ~/vm/archlinux.raw
ESP:/EFI/Linux/arch\-linux.efi	7.2.15\-arch1\-1	kernel 16844 KiB, initrd 21233 KiB
ESP:/EFI/Linux/arch\-7.2.14\-arch1\-1.efi	7.2.14\-arch1\-1	kernel 16844 KiB, initrd 70802 KiB
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gerantd (1),
.BR garnish\-vm.conf (5),
.BR seccomp (2),
.BR systemd\-boot (7),
.BR kernel\-install (8)
